{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Inside, the house smelled of woodsmoke and lavender, just as it had the first spring they were married. The kettle sat on the hob, steam curling from its spout though no one had lit the stove. Mira ran her fingers along the mantel and found the small brass bell her mother had given them, polished bright, waiting where she had always kept it. Tobias lingered in the doorway, turning his cap in his hands the way he did whenever he was unsure of his welcome. \"It's all here,\" he said. \"Every bit of it.\" Through the window they could see the lane filling with people. Agnes Halloran was carrying a tray of scones toward the chandlery, scolding someone over her shoulder. The fiddle had found its tune now, something quick and bright that set feet tapping on the cobbles. Lanterns swung from the eaves, though no one had hung them. The man from the photograph appeared at the gate and tipped his head toward the music. \"There's a place kept for you both,\" he said. \"There always was.\" Mira took off her shawl and folded it over the back of the chair by the hearth, the chair that had been hers for years before the lighthouse took them away. Then she reached for Tobias, and he came to her at last, cap forgotten on the table. Together they stepped out into the lane, into the warm press of old friends and familiar laughter. Behind them the door stayed open, and the kettle went on singing softly to the empty room."}
